# What never enters a Docker build context.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, and it is two separate reasons.
#
# THE DISCLOSURE ONE. Docker's build context ignores `.gitignore` entirely, so without this file
# `docker build .` from a working tree uploads `private/`, the operator's local state, `.venv/`,
# `.git/` and everything else beside the source. Nothing `COPY`s them today, so nothing reaches a
# layer, and that is a property of the current Dockerfiles rather than of the boundary. A future
# `COPY . .` would turn it into a disclosure with no warning. The baseline asks for the boundary
# to be declared rather than to depend on nobody adding that line.
#
# THE HONESTY ONE, which is what actually bit. Because the context ignores `.gitignore`, an image
# built on the maintainer's machine picked up files that are in no clone: the bundled corpora were
# excluded from git by an unanchored ignore rule, so the image worked here and would have shipped
# empty from CI, while both `doctor` gates in the image job grep for two lines that say nothing
# about the bundled data.
#
# The corpora are NOT excluded here, deliberately. An image is a release artefact like a wheel and
# should carry them; what was missing is a check that it does, and the image job now greps for
# that too. The exclusions below are for things no image should ever see.

# Version control and build state
.git/
.gitignore
build/
dist/
dist-ci/
dist-cleanroom/
*.egg-info/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# Environments
.venv/
venv/
docs/node_modules/

# Private and operator state, which no image has any business seeing
private/
catastrophic/
scripts/
holst/
DEFERRED.md
.claude/
evidence/

# Generated artefacts and results
results/
plots/
checkpoints/
abliterated/
out/
*.safetensors
*.gguf
*.jsonl
